"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[6490],{6490:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"obstetrics-gynaecology-test-1","slug":"obstetrics-gynaecology","subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","testNo":1,"durationMin":60,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A primigravida woman underwent a normal vaginal delivery at her home following which she presented with a perineal tear as shown below. Identify the grade of perineal tear:","options":["Grade 1","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Grade 3\\n\\nThe image showing a tear in the\xa0vaginal mucosa, perineal muscles, and more than 50% of the external anal sphincter is suggestive of a\xa0grade 3b perineal tear.\\n\\nPerineal tears are common in primigravida women undergoing vaginal delivery.\\n\\nThe first-degree tears do not require repairs but suturing of laceration prevents the vaginal orifice from becoming patulous. Second-degree tears must be immediately sutured after the delivery of the placenta to prevent the weakening of the pelvic floor and genital prolapse.\\n\\nThird and fourth-degree tears must be efficiently repaired to prevent flatus and faecal incontinence. Such tears are common in median episiotomies, incorrect usage of forceps in a persistent occipitoposterior positioned fetus, delivery of aftercoming of breech, precipitate labour and delivery of macrosomic babies.\\n\\nThe\xa0best time for repair\xa0of a complete perineal tear is\xa0within 24 hours of the injury.\xa0If not done within 24hrs, it is preferably done 3-6 months after delivery. If the perineal tear is untreated for more than 6 months, dense scar tissue forms and making the surgery difficult.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/8a34e6c105bc813a65c2.jpg"]},{"n":2,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A woman with 6 weeks of amenorrhea had a positive urine pregnancy test. The following finding was seen on transabdominal USG. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":["Anencephaly","Singleton pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Missed abortion"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Missed abortion\\n\\nThe given USG image shows an empty gestational sac which is seen in missed abortion before 12 weeks. At this gestational age,\xa0 the entire ovum is covered by the chorionic villi giving rise to the empty gestational sac.\\n\\nIf this condition occurs beyond\xa012 weeks of gestation, a dead fetus is seen on USG. This is not preceded by any\xa0warning signs and the fetus is not expelled outside spontaneously.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/cfe5640684a1139f3c66.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e79693998c768f131f03.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9c056fa3358a30e0d27c.jpg"}]},{"n":3,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A woman presents to the outpatient department with complaints of amenorrhoea for the past year. She has a history of postpartum hemorrhage and lactational failure. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":["Sheehan\'s syndrome","Hyperthyroidism","Prolactinoma","Pituitary apoplexy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Sheehan\'s syndrome\\n\\nThe clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of\xa0Sheehan\'s syndrome.\\n\\nSheehan syndrome\xa0also called post-partum pituitary necrosis refers to the necrosis of the pituitary gland following significant post-partum bleeding, hypovolemia, and shock.\xa0It may present acutely as an endocrine emergency or present chronically.The late presentation has the following features:\\n\\n• Lactation failure - due to prolactin deficiency\\n• Amenorrhea - due to anovulation caused by reduced gonadotropin activity\\n• Hypothyroidism - due to decreased TSH secretion\\n• Adrenal insufficiency - due to low ACTH\\n\\nOption B: Hyperthyroidism can cause oligomenorrhoea and amenorrhea but is likely to have symptoms of hyperthyroidism like\xa0weight loss, palpitations, tremors, heat intolerance, dyspnea on exertion, diarrhoea and muscle weakness.\\n\\nOption C: Prolactinoma\xa0or pituitary adenoids is the condition of hyperprolactinemia diagnosed by a prolactin level >200 μg/L. It usually presents with infertility, amenorrhea, and galactorrhea.\\n\\nOption D: Pituitary apoplexy is a condition in which there is a\xa0haemorrhage or infarction\xa0of the pituitary gland. This disorder commonly occurs in a pre-existing pituitary adenoma. This patient does not have any signs of adenoma and a history of PPH makes\xa0Sheehan syndrome more likely.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 32-year-old woman in labor is referred to a tertiary care center. On PV examination, the resident notes that the fetus is in brow presentation, and the largest diameter of the fetal skull is engaged. Identify this diameter.","options":["Mentovertical","Biparietal","Submentovertical","Occipitofrontal"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Mentovertical\\n\\nIn brow presentation, the mentovertical diameter is engaged which is\xa0the\xa0longest diameter of the fetal skull. Normally, it measures\xa014 cm.\\n\\nThe mentovertical diameter extends from the midpoint of the chin to the highest point on the sagittal suture.\\n\\nEngaging diameters of the fetal skull vary with the attitude of the fetal head as given below:\\n\\nDiameters\\nAttitude of the head\\n\\nSuboccipitobregmatic\\nComplete flexion\\n\\nSuboccipito-frontal\\nIncomplete flexion\\n\\nOccipitofrontal\\nMarked deflexion\\n\\nMento-vertical\\nPartial extension\\n\\nSubmentovertical\\nIncomplete extension\\n\\nSubmentobregmatic\\nComplete extension","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A type 1 diabetic mother is on magnesium sulfate infusion post-cesarean section for preeclampsia. She develops delirium and is drowsy. She has a respiratory rate of 10/min, random blood glucose level of 240 mg/dL, oliguria, and bilaterally absent knee reflex. What is the cause of her condition?","options":["Magnesium sulfate toxicity","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Eclampsia","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Magnesium sulfate toxicity\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario and examination finding of bilaterally absent knee reflex\xa0following magnesium sulfate (MgSO4) administration for preeclampsia suggest the cause of her condition to be magnesium sulfate\xa0toxicity.\\n\\nMgSO4 is used as an anticonvulsant in the management of eclampsia and as prophylaxis in severe preeclampsia. It can also be used as a tocolytic to stop preterm labor.\xa0The therapeutic level of magnesium in eclampsia is 4–7 mEq/L.\\n\\nPatients must be monitored for magnesium toxicity:\\n\\n• Loss of deep tendon reflexes\\n• Decreased respiratory rate <12/min\\n• Urine output\xa0<30 mL/h\\n• Chest pain and heart block\\n• Visual changes, flushing, muscle paralysis, and drowsiness\\n\\nMeasurement of serum magnesium levels is indicated to monitor magnesium toxicity if serum creatinine >1.0 mg/dL.\\n\\nManagement of magnesium toxicity is as follows:\\n\\n• Stop magnesium therapy\\n• Estimation of serum magnesium and creatinine levels\\n• Intravenous injection of calcium gluconate 10 mL\xa0(10% solution)\\n\\nThe two commonly used MgSO4 regimens are described below:\\n\\nRegimen\\n\\nLoading Dose\\n\\nMaintenance Dose\\n\\nIntramuscular (Pritchard)\\n\\n4 g (20% solution) IV over 3–5 minutes, followed by 10 g (50%) deep IM\xa0(5 g in each buttock)\\n\\n5 g (50%) IM 4 hourly in the alternate buttock\\n\\nIntravenous (Zuspan or Sibai)\\n\\n4 g IV slow over 15–20 minute\\n\\n1–2 g/h IV infusion\\n\\nRepeated/maintenance dose IM injections of magnesium sulfate are continued only under the following conditions:\\n\\n• The patellar reflex is present\\n• Respiration is not depressed\\n• Urine output >30 mL/h\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Pregnant women with diabetic ketoacidosis present with hyperventilation (Kussmaul breathing) and their blood glucose level is around 380 mg/dL.\\n\\nOption C: Eclampsia\xa0patients present with generalized tonic-clonic convulsions.\\n\\nOption D: Diabetes insipidus\xa0patients present with polyuria and polydipsia.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 58-year-old woman is being evaluated for bleeding per vagina. She attained menopause at the age of 47. Which of the following is least likely to be a cause of post-menopausal bleeding?","options":["Genital tract trauma","Ovarian Follicular cyst","Endometrial carcinoma","Granulosa cell tumor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ovarian Follicular cyst\\n\\nAmong the given options,\xa0ovarian follicular cysts are least likely to be a cause of post-menopausal bleeding.\\n\\nOvarian follicular cysts\xa0are usually asymptomatic unless they rupture, hemorrhage, or the ovary undergoes torsion which would manifest as an acute abdomen. They occur when there is the failure of absorption of fluid in an incompletely developed follicle or anovulation.\\n\\nPostmenopausal bleeding is diagnosed when there is bleeding per vagina after 6 months of amenorrhoea after the age of 40 (menopausal age).\\n\\nCauses of postmenopausal bleeding include:\\n\\n• Genital tract trauma (Option A)\\n• Cervical erosion, inflammation, polyp, ulcer, or cervical carcinoma.\\n• Uterus - endometritis, endometrial hyperplasia, endometrial carcinoma (Option C), polyp, or sarcoma\\n• Fallopian tube malignancy\\n• Ovarian tumors such as Brenner tumor, granulosa cell tumor (Option D)\\n• Hypertension and blood dyscrasia\\n• Urinary tract trauma, bladder carcinoma\\n• Hemorrhoid, anal fissures, and rectal cancers confused as bleeding per vagina\\n• Use of estrogens as hormone replacement therapy, tamoxifen.\\n\\nThe image below gives a summary of the evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding, as per the FOGSI guidelines.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/6339878acf6ec17849f2.jpg"}]},{"n":7,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Match the following.\\n\\n1. Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome\\na. Normal uterus and vagina, streak ovaries\\n\\n2. Turner\'s syndrome\\nb.Absent or rudimentary uterus and upper vagina, normal ovaries\\n\\n3.\xa0Swyer’s Syndrome\\nc. Absent uterus and upper vagina, cryptorchid testis\\n\\n4. Mullerian agenesis\\nd.\xa0Normal uterus and vagina, with streak gonads, and risk of testicular tumor","options":["1-b, 2-a, 4-c, 3-d","1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c","1-a, 2-d, 3-b, 4-c","1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b\\n\\nThe correct match is\xa01-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b.\\n\\n1. Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome\\n\\nc. Absent uterus and upper vagina, and\xa0cryptorchid testis\\n\\n2. Turner\'s syndrome\\na. Normal uterus and vagina, streak ovaries\\n\\n3.\xa0Swyer’s Syndrome\\nd.\xa0Normal uterus and vagina, with streak gonads, and risk of testicular tumor\\n\\n4. Mullerian agenesis\\n\\nb.Absent or rudimentary uterus and upper vagina, normal ovaries","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 55-year-old woman is diagnosed to have cervical cancer. MRI and PET-CT imaging reveal tumour extension up to the pelvic wall and involvement of the para-aortic lymph nodes. It also shows features suggestive of hydronephrosis. Which stage is it according to the FIGO staging of cervical cancer?\\n\\nPET-CT - Positron Emission Tomography/Computed Tomography\\nFIGO - International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics","options":["Stage III C1","Stage III C2","Stage IV A","Stage III B"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Stage III C2\\n\\nCervical cancer involving the para-aortic lymph nodes is classified as Stage III C2 according to FIGO staging.\\n\\nCervical cancer is the most common gynecological cancer. The FIGO staging uses clinical, radiological, or pathological findings to stage cervical cancer from Stage I to IV.\\n\\nStage III cervical carcinoma involves the lower 1/3rd of the vagina, with or without pelvic wall extension, hydronephrosis, and lymph node involvement. It is further staged as follows:\\n\\n• Stage III A: Lower 1/3rd of the vagina involvement, no pelvic wall extension\\n• Stage III B:\xa0Pelvic wall involvement with or without hydronephrosis or a non-functioning kidney\\n• Stage III C:\xa0Involvement of the pelvic and/or paraaortic lymph nodes\\n\\n• Stage III C1: Metastasis to\xa0pelvic lymph node only\\n• Stage III C2: Metastasis to para-aortic lymph nodes\\n\\nNote: If the carcinoma had extended beyond the true pelvis, involving adjacent pelvic organs like the bladder or rectum, it would\'ve been classified as Stage IV A.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":9,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Which of the following is false regarding the marked incision shown below?","options":["Location is 3 cm above the pubic symphysis","Lower incidence of incisional hernias as compared to vertical incisions","The rectus abdominis muscles are transected","Follows Langer lines of skin tension"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. The rectus abdominis muscles are transected\\n\\nThe incision marked is the Pfannenstiel incision where the rectus muscles are not transected. The rectus sheath is incised transversely and\xa0rectus muscles are separated\xa0in the middle to gain entry to the peritoneum.\\n\\nPfannenstiel incision is the most common incision made in cesarean delivery.\\u0016It follows Langer lines of skin tension. A transverse and slightly curvilinear incision is made in the skin and subcutaneous tissue 3 cm above the superior border of the pubic symphysis. This is stretched laterally for 12-15 cm to accommodate the delivery of the fetus. Following this, the rectus sheath is incised transversely and rectus muscles are separated in the middle to gain entry to the peritoneum.\\n\\nCompared to vertical incisions, the Pfannenstiel incision offers superior cosmesis and lower incisional hernia rates.\\n\\nThe\xa0Maylard incision\xa0(the unmarked incision in the image) differs mainly from the Pfannenstiel in that the bellies of the rectus abdominis muscles are transected horizontally to widen the operating space.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/d74ce855b1c541848982.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e53ceed4115bb3a5dd53.jpg"}]},{"n":10,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 35-year-old woman conceived after being treated for infertility. Her USG scan is given below. What is her diagnosis?","options":["Singleton pregnancy with uterine septum","Monochorionic diamniotic twins","Dichorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Dichorionic diamniotic twins\\n\\nThe given image shows the twin peak sign which is seen in dichorionic diamniotic twins. This is also known as the\xa0lambda sign or delta sign.\\n\\nIn dichorionic twins, the intervening membrane between the twins has 4 layers i.e. 2 chorions and 2 amnions. The placenta protrudes from the base to the space between the 2 amnion layers. This gives rise to the twin peak sign i.e. triangle with the base at placenta and apex in the intervening membrane.\\n\\nSonographic assessment of dichorionicity is done by observing the following:\\n\\n• Number of placental masses - 2\\n• Presence of intervening membrane - twin peak or lambda or delta sign\\n• Thickness of intervening membranes ≥ 2mm\\n\\nThe best time of delivery of dichorionic diamniotic twins is 38 weeks.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/cbd74e07c973355d9452.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/281bfd876369dee8d9b6.jpg"}]},{"n":11,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"You are a medical officer on camp duty seeing patients who have come for the removal of contraceptive devices. You notice the following contraceptive devices being used. Which of the following is incorrect about these devices?\\n\\nPelvic Inflammatory Disease - PID","options":["Can be used as an emergency contraceptive","Can be used in patients with mennorrhagia","Contraindicated in patients with fibroids","Functions by causing an inflammatory response within the uterus"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Can be used in patients with mennorrhagia\\n\\nThe image shows types of Copper T. The statement that it \'can be used in patients with menorrhagia\' is incorrect about the copper T. Copper T\'s are contraindicated in patients with menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea, and anemia as it can worsen the condition.\\n\\nCopper T is a type of intrauterine contraception device (IUCD). They provide long-term, reversible method of contraception.\\n\\nTypes:\\n\\n• Medicated IUCD\'s such as progestasert, levonova, and mirena which release hormones for contraception.\\n• Unmedicated IUCD\'s such as the copper T, in order to induce contraception.\\n\\nThe copper T functions by causing an inflammatory response (Option D) within the uterus which leads to spermicide. The same response also makes it difficult for blastocyst implantation.\\n\\nIUCDs can be placed in women who have a low risk of sexually transmitted diseases, are multiparous, monogamous, and want long-term contraception without permanent sterilization. These have the advantage of not having to take a daily oral contraceptive pill.\\n\\nThe copper T can also be used as emergency contraception (Option A) and left inside the uterus long term for contraception.\\n\\nIt is contraindicated in the following situations:\\n\\n• Suspected pregnancy\\n• Fibroid uterus (Option C) as there will be structural issues\\n• Pelvic inflammatory disease\xa0or lower genital tract infections\\n• Menorrhagia or dysmenorrhea\\n• Anemia\\n• Uncontrolled diabetes due to increased risk of infection\\n• Previous ectopic pregnancy\\n• Scarred uterus\\n• Abnormally shaped uterus\\n\\nIn addition, hormonal IUCDs should not be given in women with history of breast cancer.\\n\\nThe following image shows the Nova T. It has silver in addition to the copper wire which makes its lifespan 5 years.\\n\\nThe copper T 380A is also known as Paraguard. It has a lifespan of 10 years. It has 380 mm2 surface area spread out across the stem and the sleeves.\\n\\nThe multiload 375 has 375 mm2 of copper wire wrapped around the stem. It has to be replaced every 5 years. It does not have an introducer or plunger.\\n\\nThe copper T 200 has 215mm2 of copper around the stem. It has a polyethylene frame with monofilaments attached to the end of the stem. The threads are used for removal. Barium sulfate is added to the stem to increase radioopacity when performing xrays. It has a lifespan of 3 years.\\n\\nThe following images represent the copper T 380A, multiload 375, and copper T 200.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/f0dfc6e75eaf307b0f2f.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9b4fd7ec95a87cfa6948.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/992d0bb6bf0200b74698.jpg"}]},{"n":12,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A primigravida at 32 weeks of gestation was admitted for management of preterm labour. Which of the following tocolytics would be preferred for this?","options":["Glyceryl trinitrate","Nifedipine","Magnesium sulphate","Ritodrine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Nifedipine\\n\\nNifedipine is the preferred tocolytic as it has minimum maternal side effects.\\n\\nAccording to ACOG guidelines, the use of tocolytics in preterm labour does not prolong gestation but may delay delivery upto 48 hours. Hence, short-term therapy with tocolytics is given for 1-3 days to delay labour for the action of corticosteroid therapy or to transfer the patient to a tertiary centre (with NICU).\\n\\nTocolytics used in preterm labour and their side effects:\\n\\nTocolytics\\nMaternal side effects\\nFetal/ neonatal side effects\\n\\nBeta-adrenergic agonists:\\n\\n• Ritodrine\\n• Terbutaline\\n• Isoxsuprine\\n\\nHeadache, palpitations, tachycardia, hypotension, pulmonary edema, cardiac failure, ARDS\\n\\nFetal heart failure, intrauterine death\\n\\nNeonatal hypoglycemia, intraventricular haemorrhage\\n\\nCalcium channel blockers:\\n\\n• Nifedipine\\n• Nicardipine\\n• Verapamil\\n\\nHypotension, headache, flushing, nausea\\n-\\n\\nOxytocin antagonists:\\n\\n• Atosiban\\n\\n(not approved for use in India)\\n\\nNausea, vomiting, chest pain\\n-\\n\\nMagnesium sulphate\\nFlushing, perspiration, headache, muscle weakness, respiratory paralysis\\nNeonatal cardiac arrest, lethargy, hypotonia, respiratory depression\\n\\nCOX inhibitors:\\n\\n• Indomethacin\\n• Sulindac\\n\\nHeartburn, asthma, GI bleeding, thrombocytopenia, renal injury, platelet dysfunction, oligohydramnios\\nConstriction of ductus arteriosus, pulmonary hypertension, intrauterine growth restriction","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A pregnant woman presents at 33 weeks gestation with intermittent uterine contractions. On per vaginal examination, the cervix is 80% effaced and 2.5cm dilated. To allow fetal lung maturity, what is the dose of betamethasone to be given?","options":["12mg, 12 hourly, 4 doses IV","12 mg, 24 hours apart, 2 doses IM","6 mg, 24 hours apart, 2 doses IV","6 mg, 12 hourly, 4 doses IM"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 12 mg, 24 hours apart, 2 doses IM\\n\\nTo allow fetal lung maturity, the dose of betamethasone\xa0to be given is 12mg IM 24 hourly 2 doses.\\n\\nAntenatal corticosteroids are recommended in a pregnancy less than 34 weeks to allow fetal lung maturation and to reduce the incidence of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH), and necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC).\\n\\n• Betamethasone is the steroid of choice and it becomes useful if the delivery is delayed beyond 48 hours of the first dose with the effect lasting for about 18 days.\\n• Dexamethasone is another steroid that can be given in a dose of\xa06 mg 12 hourly 4 doses IM.\\n\\nPreterm labor is the onset of labor before 37 completed weeks of pregnancy (less than 259 days of gestation). It can occur in patients with a history of prior preterm birth, multiple pregnancies, and\xa0genital tract infection.\xa0Evaluation of cervical anatomy\xa0is the\xa0most important step\xa0in\xa0women with a\xa0history of preterm labor to look for cervical incompetence.\\n\\nIt is diagnosed by:\\n\\n• Uterus - regular\xa0contractions\xa0at least one in\xa0every 10 minutes, or 4 contractions in 20 minutes or 8 contractions in 60 min with/without pain.\\n• Cervix -\xa0dilatation >2 cm\xa0and\xa0effacement >80%\\n• TVS - length of\xa0cervix <2.5 cm\xa0and\xa0funneling\xa0of the internal os\xa0>1 cm\\n• Pelvic pressure, backache, vaginal discharge, or bleeding PV\\n\\nThe\xa0best predictors\xa0of preterm labor in a woman with a previous history of preterm birth are\xa0fetal fibronectin\xa0>50ng/ml and\xa0cervical length\xa0<2.5cm on transvaginal ultrasound.\\n\\nManagement includes short-term therapy with\xa0tocolytics, given in conjunction with steroids for 1-3 days to\xa0delay labor\xa0for at least\xa048 hours\xa0or to transfer the patient to a tertiary center (centers with NICU). The tocolytics include terbutaline, indomethacin, nifedipine, and atosiban.\xa0Contraindications\xa0to\xa0tocolysis include chorioamnionitis, preeclampsia/eclampsia, fetal distress, pregnancy >34 weeks, active labor, and placental abruption.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":14,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 24-year-old lady who underwent suction evacuation for molar pregnancy visits you on postoperative week 8 for her follow-up. Which of the following is not done during the follow-up of this patient?\\n\\n1. Ask her for a history of any irregular vaginal bleeding, cough, or\xa0breathlessness.\\n2. Perform an abdominal examination to look for the size of the uterus and the ovaries.\\n3. Perform a pelvic examination to look for sub-urethral nodules\\n4. Request for a serum hCG value,\xa0a pelvic ultrasound, and chest X-ray PA view\\n5. Insert a copper-T device to avoid pregnancy","options":["5 only","1, 5","3, 4","2, 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 5 only\\n\\nInsertion of a copper-T device to avoid pregnancy is not done during follow-up of this patient.\\n\\nIntrauterine devices are contraindicated\xa0post-evacuation of molar pregnancy due to the\xa0increased risk of perforation.\xa0Intrauterine devices and progestogen-only pills cause irregular bleeding and are best avoided as they lead to\xa0suspicion of malignancy. Safer contraceptive choices for such patients include combined oral pills, injectable DMPA(depot-medroxyprogesterone acetate), progestin implant, barrier methods, and sterilization.\\n\\nContraception has to be adopted by all patients who underwent suction curettage\xa0until HCG levels become undetectable and remain the same for a period of 6 months. This serves to avoid pregnancy and confusion regarding the source of HCG if there is a subsequent rise. After beta HCG levels have become normal,\xa0subsequent conception must be avoided for\xa0one year(preferably 2 years).\xa0Pregnancy should also be avoided for 1 year after the stoppage of chemotherapy because of the teratogenic effect of drugs.\\n\\nAll patients should be followed-up for 2 years because choriocarcinoma if it occurs, develops within this period of the evacuation of the mole. Commonly, the circulating levels of\xa0βhCG decrease or becomes negative within 4\xa0to 8 weeks\xa0post evacuation. Once negative,\xa0the patient is usually followed up monthly for 6 months, then 3 monthly in the first year, and 6 monthly in the second year.\\n\\nDuring follow-up visits, history should be taken about\xa0symptoms like\xa0irregular bleeding (choriocarcinoma),\xa0presence of nodules, cough, breathlessness, and hemoptysis (metastasis). An abdominal examination should be done to check for\xa0involution of the uterus and ovarian size. A pelvic examination is done to look for sub-urethral nodules (anterior vaginal wall).\\n\\nLab investigations\xa0must include routine investigations like haemogram, renal function, liver function test, and urine routine. Specific investigations include\xa0thyroid function tests\xa0and\xa0serum\xa0βhCG (by radio-immunoassay).\xa0Persistently high values indicate pregnancy or invasive mole.\\n\\nRadiological investigations should\xa0include a chest X-ray PA view to look for lung metastases (classic\xa0cannon-ball appearance can be seen).\xa0Pelvic ultrasound is also done for surveillance. Myometrial or suburethral nodules or hypervascularity may be a predictor of subsequent neoplasia.\\n\\nThe given image shows the classical\xa0cannon-ball\xa0appearance on the chest X-ray.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f448d8578d82d1801ad6.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Match the best parameter to determine gestational age by ultrasound in the given period (period of gestation determined by last menstrual period).\\n\\nPeriod of gestation\\n\\nParameter\\n\\n1. \xa016 weeks\\n\\nA: Crown-rump length\\n\\n2. 32 weeks\\n\\nB: Biparietal diameter\\n\\n3. 10 weeks\\n\\nC: Femur length","options":["1-B, 2-C, 3-A","1-B, 2-A, 3-C","1-C, 2-A, 3-B","1-A, 2-C, 3-B"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-B, 2-C, 3-A\\n\\nThe correctly matched pair is 1-B, 2-C, 3-A.\\n\\nBest parameters for estimation of fetal age\\n\\nTrimester\\n\\nParameter\\n\\nFirst\\n\\nCrown-rump length (CRL)\\n\\nSecond\\n\\nBiparietal diameter (BPD)\\n\\nThird\\n\\nFemur length\\n\\nOverall\\n\\nCrown-rump length (CRL)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Match the following patients with Uterine prolapse with their best treatment option.\\n\\nAge/condition\\nManagement\\n\\n1. Nulliparous women with desire for future pregnancy\\n2. 45-year-old with no desire for menstruation/pregnancy\\n3. 30-year-old in puerperial period\\n4. 38-year-old with family complete but desires for menstruation.\\n\\n1. Fothergill\'s operation\\n2. Cervicopexy\\n3. Vaginal hysterectomy with pelvic floor repair\\n4. \xa0Ring pessary","options":["1-iii, 2-i, 3-ii, 4-iv","1-iv, 2-ii, 3-i, 4-iii","1-ii, 2-iii, 3-iv, 4-i","1-iv, 2-iii, 3-ii, 4-i"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1-ii, 2-iii, 3-iv, 4-i\\n\\nOption C is correct.\\n\\nAge/condition\\nManagement\\n\\n1)\xa0Nulliparous women with a\xa0desire for future pregnancy\\n\\nii. \xa0Cervicopexy\\n\\n2)\xa045-year-old with no desire for menstruation/pregnancy\\n\\niii. \xa0Vaginal hysterectomy with pelvic floor repair\\n\\n3) 30-year-old in puerperial period\\n\\niv. \xa0Ring pessary\\n\\n4)\xa038-year-old with family complete but desires for menstruation.\\n\\ni. \xa0Fothergill\'s operation\\n\\nSling operations\xa0are recommended in cases of\xa0nulliparous prolapse for those who wish to retain menstrual and child-bearing function. Mackenrodt’s and uterosacral ligaments of the uterus are strengthened, by using nylon or Dacron tapes as slings. Abdominal sling operations include\xa0abdominal wall cervicopexy, Shirodkar’s\xa0sling operation,\xa0Khanna’s\xa0abdominal sling operation,\xa0Virkud\'s\xa0sling operation, and Joshi\'s\xa0sling operation.\\n\\nVaginal hysterectomy with pelvic floor repair\xa0is the ideal management of genital prolapse in women >40 years of age, those who have completed their families and do\xa0not wish to preserve menstrual and child-bearing function.\\n\\nA ring pessary is used for patients who cannot be surgically managed, such as\xa0pregnant\xa0patients, patients in\xa0puerperium, and very\xa0elderly\xa0women unfit for surgery. It is used in adjunct with pelvic floor physiotherapy. Pessaries provide mechanical support to the pelvic organs. The first step after fitting the pessary to check if the pessary is adequately retained is to ask the patient to perform\xa0Valsalva\'s manoeuvre\xa0or\xa0cough\xa0and see if the pessary is in place. After being placed, they need to be\xa0changed\xa0every\xa03 months.\\n\\nFothergill\'s repair\xa0also known as\xa0Manchester\'s\xa0operation is suitable for women\xa0under 40 years\xa0who are desirous of retaining their menstrual and reproductive functions. It involves anterior colporrhaphy with\xa0amputation\xa0of the\xa0cervix. The mackenrodt ligaments are sutured in the front of the cervix and colpoperineorrhaphy is done. It increases the risk of\xa0cervical incompetence\xa0leading to\xa0recurrent abortions\xa0and\xa0preterm deliveries.\xa0Shirodkar\'s modification\xa0of Fothergill\'s repair\xa0avoids cervical amputation,\xa0hence subsequent pregnancy complications are avoided.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/27ca8b396cac859741a2.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9af8887a34860f14d2b8.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fb0d360217debdf3e0ca.jpg"}]},{"n":17,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A grand multipara had continuous, moderate vaginal bleeding immediately after delivery. What could be the most probable cause of bleeding in this patient?","options":["Genital tract trauma","Uterine inversion","Uterine atony","Retained tissues"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Uterine atony\\n\\nUterine atony is the most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage in a grand multipara.\\n\\nUterine atony is the failure of the uterus to contract sufficiently after delivery. Contraction helps to arrest bleeding from vessels at the placental implantation site.\\n\\nTypes of PPH:\\n\\n1. Primary/early: Haemorrhage occurring within 24 hours of childbirth. Uterine atony is the most common cause of primary PPH.\\n2. Secondary/late: Hemorrhage occurring after 24 hours and up to 12 weeks postpartum. Retained placenta is the most common cause.\\n\\nThe risk factors for atonic uterus include:\\n\\n1. Primiparity or grand multiparity\\n2. Previous PPH\\n3. Overdistended uterus as in multiple pregnancies, hydramnios, macrosomia\\n4. Precipitate labour\\n5. Prolonged labour\\n6. Uterine malformations\\n7. Uterine fibroid\\n8. Induction of labour with prostaglandins or oxytocin\\n9. Placental abnormalities like placenta previa, accreta, increta, percreta, or abruptio placenta\\n10. Obesity\\n11. Age >40 years\\n\\nNote: Uterine inversion occurs rarely and causes secondary PPH.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":18,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Which of the following arteries is ligated first during surgical management of postpartum hemorrhage?","options":["Anastomosis of uterine and ovarian arteries","Ovarian artery","Uterine artery","Internal iliac artery"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Uterine artery\\n\\nThe uterine artery is the first artery that is ligated during the surgical management of postpartum hemorrhage(PPH).\\n\\nIf PPH\xa0is due to a flabby uterus on examination; then uterine massage is done to improve atony. Methergine 0.2 mg and oxytocin are administered to promote the contraction of the atonic uterus. Also, the uterus has to be explored for any retained membranes or cotyledons of the placenta.\\n\\nInitial resuscitative measures include the infusion of 2L of normal saline (crystalloids) through large-bore IV cannulas. Relevant blood investigations such as blood count, grouping, cross-matching, LFT, RFT, and coagulation studies are done.\xa020 units of oxytocin in 1L of normal saline at the rate of 60 drops/minute is started along with blood transfusion. The bladder is catheterized.\\n\\nMedical management of PPH includes carbetocin, a long-acting oxytocin analog, which is also effective. PGF-2 alpha, misoprostol(1000 micrograms), tranexamic acid, and calcium gluconate IV 1g slowly are given in refractory cases.\\n\\nUterine packing with balloon tamponade, foley catheter, and Sengstaken-Blakemore tube is used as a first-line surgical intervention for atonic PPH.\\nSurgical methods for the management of atonic PPH in sequence are discussed below:\\n\\n1. B -Lynch compression sutures act as a tamponade to control hemorrhage.\\n2. The ascending branch of the uterine artery is ligated next.\\n3. Uterine and ovarian arteries anastomosis are ligated if the bleeding persists.\\n4. Ligation of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and angiographic embolization is done if the above-mentioned methods failed.\\n5. Following this, a hysterectomy is the last resort.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":19,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A middle-aged woman, who is on the workup for infertility and menstrual irregularities underwent hysterosalpingography(HSG ). What features on HSG would have helped the doctor to make a diagnosis of genital tuberculosis?\\n\\n1- Rigid non-peristaltic pipe-like tube.\\n2- Wedge-shaped defect at the uterine isthmus.\\n3- Tobacco-pouch appearance of hydrosalpinx.\\n4- Golf club appearance in the ampullary portion of the fallopian tube.","options":["1, 3, 4","3 and 4","2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3, 4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 3, 4\\n\\nA wedge shaped defect at the uterine isthmus is seen in\xa0patients with cesarean section history with a uterine scar and not in genital tuberculosis.\\n\\nHysterosalpingography features of\xa0genital tuberculosis(tubercular salpingitis) are:\\n\\n• Rigid non-peristaltic pipe-like tube (Lead pipe appearance)\\n• Beading and variation in filling defect\\n• Calcification of the tube\\n• Jagged fluffiness of the tubal outline\\n• Tobacco-pouch appearance of hydrosalpinx and pyosalpinx\\n• Golf club appearance with slight or moderate dilatation of the ampullary portion of the fallopian tube.\\n• Rosette type appearance secondary to multiple small diverticular like out pouching surrounding the ampulla produced by caseous ulceration.\\n\\nTuberculous tubes:\\n\\nNote :\xa0HSG\xa0should not be performed\xa0in\xa0a\xa0suspected case of genital tuberculosis\xa0as it causes dissemination of the infection. This further increases the damage to the fallopian tubes.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2a5cf0e2240e9b81c7ec.png"}]},{"n":20,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"When does the ovary contain the maximum number of oogonia?","options":["7th month of intrauterine life","At birth","5th month of intrauterine life","3rd month of intrauterine life"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 5th month of intrauterine life\\n\\nThe ovary contains the maximum number of oogonia at the\xa05th month of intrauterine life.\xa0The number of oogonia is estimated to be 7 million.\\n\\nOogenesis is the process wherein oogonia differentiate into mature oocytes. It begins before birth. At birth, there are\xa0approximately 2 million primordial follicles. At the initiation of puberty, the follicle count is less than 400,000,\xa0of which less than 500 are destined to ovulate.\xa0Every month, 15–20 follicles are released, out of which one matures to form the ovum.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/93e735102ce8c45fba7e.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A G2P1L1 woman with a previous history of preterm delivery presents at 32 weeks with intermittent uterine contractions. To allow for fetal lung maturity, what is the dose of betamethasone to be given?","options":["6 mg, 24 hours apart, 2 doses IV","6 mg, 12 hourly, 4 doses IM","12 mg, 24 hours apart, 2 doses IM","12mg, 12 hourly, 4 doses IV"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 12 mg, 24 hours apart, 2 doses IM\\n\\nTo allow for fetal lung maturity, the dose of betamethasone\xa0to be given is 12mg IM 24 hourly 2 doses.\\n\\nAntenatal corticosteroids are recommended in a pregnancy less than 34 weeks to allow fetal lung maturation and to reduce the incidence of respiratory distress syndrome(RDS), intraventricular hemorrhage(IVH), and necrotizing enterocolitis(NEC).\\n\\nBetamethasone is the steroid of choice and it becomes useful if the delivery is delayed beyond 48 hours of the first dose with the effect lasting for about 18 days.\xa0Dexamethasone is another steroid that can be given in a dose of\xa06 mg 12 hourly 4 doses IM.\\n\\nShort-term therapy with\xa0tocolytics\xa0is given in conjunction with steroids for 1-3 days to\xa0delay labour\xa0for at least\xa048 hours\xa0or to transfer the patient to a tertiary centre (with NICU). The drugs include terbutaline, indomethacin, nifedipine, and atosiban.\xa0Contraindications\xa0to\xa0tocolysis include chorioamnionitis, preeclampsia/eclampsia, fetal distress, pregnancy >34 weeks, active labor, and placental abruption.\\n\\nPreterm labour is the onset of labour before 37 completed weeks of pregnancy (less than 259 days of gestation). It can occur in patients with a history of prior preterm birth, multiple pregnancies and\xa0genital tract infection.\xa0Evaluation of cervical anatomy\xa0is the\xa0most important step\xa0in\xa0women with a\xa0history of preterm labour to look for cervical incompetence.\\n\\nFeatures\xa0of preterm labour include\xa0regular uterine\xa0contractions\xa0at least once\xa0every 10 minutes, with/without pain, cervical\xa0dilatation >2 cm\xa0and\xa0effacement >80%\xa0and\xa0length of\xa0cervix <2.5 cm\xa0and\xa0funnelling\xa0of the internal os\xa0>1 cm.\\n\\nThe\xa0best predictors\xa0of preterm labor in a woman with a previous history of preterm birth are\xa0fetal fibronectin\xa0>50ng/ml and\xa0cervical length\xa0<2.5cm on transvaginal ultrasound.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"In a woman diagnosed with infertility, which of the following antihormonal substance can be used to induce ovulation?","options":["Mifepristone","Tamoxifen","Clomiphene citrate","Danazol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Clomiphene citrate\\n\\nClomiphene citrate is an antihormonal substance used to induce ovulation in a patient diagnosed with infertility. Ovulation can be induced by giving clomiphene citrate 50 mg daily, starting from day 2 to day 6 of the menstrual cycle for 5 days.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":23,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A young female patient is undergoing evaluation for infertility. The cervical mucus shows the following appearance on microscopy. During which phase of the menstrual cycle is the given finding seen?","options":["Just after the ovulation","Progestogenic phase of menstrual cycle","Any phase of menstrual cycle","Estrogenic phase of the menstrual cycle"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Estrogenic phase of the menstrual cycle\\n\\nThe image shows a characteristic ferning pattern of cervical mucus seen during the\xa0estrogenic phase of the menstrual cycle.\\n\\nThe characteristics of cervical mucous changes under the influence of estrogen and progesterone. During the estrogenic phase of the menstrual cycle, the cervical mucus is thin and stretchy due to the presence of sodium\xa0chloride in the mucus\xa0which shows the characteristic pattern seen above. This pattern disappears once ovulation has taken place due to the activity of progesterone. If previously present, its disappearance is presumptive evidence of corpus luteum activity.\\n\\nAt the time of ovulation, the mucus becomes thin, watery, and clear that can be stretched up to 10cm. This is called spinnbarkeit or thread test and is presumptive of ovulation. During the progesterone phase, the mucus becomes thick\xa0and tenacious, impermeable\xa0to the sperms.\\n\\nOther signs of ovulation are\\n\\n• An\xa0increase in basal body temperature(BBT)\xa0after ovulation due to the\xa0thermogenic action\xa0of the hormone\xa0progesterone.\\n• Urinary\xa0LH peak\xa0(increase in LH) is usually seen\xa01 day prior to ovulation.\\n• The growth of ovarian follicles is\xa020 mm or more\xa0in size on USG at the time of ovulation.\\n\\nThe menstrual cycle consists of three phases: the\xa0menstrual phase, the\xa0proliferative phase, and the\xa0secretory, or progestational, phase\\n\\nThe menstrual phase\xa0is characterized by the discharge of blood and endometrial debris from the vagina due to the withdrawal of progesterone and the degeneration of the corpus luteum (CL).\\n\\nThe\xa0proliferative phase\xa0is estrogen-dominant and lasts from the end of menstruation to ovulation. It results in the proliferation of endometrial glands and thickened endometrium.\\n\\nThe secretory\xa0or progestational phase is progesterone dominant which converts the thickened, estrogen-primed endometrium to a richly vascularized, glycogen-filled tissue for early nourishment of a developing embryo. If fertilization and implantation do not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates and a new follicular phase and menstrual phase begin again.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/6a294b3562301884e41d.jpg"]},{"n":24,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"During genetic counseling, a pregnant woman states that both she and her husband have sickle cell trait. You offer the option of chorionic villus sampling for prenatal diagnosis. What is the best time to perform this procedure?","options":["6-8 weeks","7-9 weeks","11-13 weeks","9-10 weeks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 11-13 weeks\\n\\nThe best time to perform chorionic villous sampling is 11-13 weeks.\\n\\nChorionic villus sampling:\\n\\nStudy material\\n\\nChorionic villi from trophoblastic cells\\n\\nTime\\n\\n10 to 13 weeks\xa0(transcervically)\\n\\n10 weeks to term (transabdominally)\\n\\nIndications\\n\\nTo detect chromosomal abnormalities such as Down syndrome.\\n\\nTo detect inborn errors of metabolism like phenylketonuria.\\n\\nFor DNA analysis in hemoglobinopathies, thalassemia, and sickle cell anemia.\\n\\nComplications\\n\\nAbortion (CVS> Amniocentesis)\\n\\nLimb reduction defects and oro-mandibular defects\xa0(when done before 10 weeks)\\n\\nDrawback\\n\\nNot suitable for the detection of neural tube defects.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":25,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Given below are the four causes of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) with the corresponding class of a PALM-COEIN classification. Which is incorrect?","options":["Hemophilia A: AUB-C","Submucosal fibroid: AUB-L (SM)","Intrauterine contraceptive devices: AUB-I","Polycystic ovarian disease: AUB-P"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Polycystic ovarian disease: AUB-P\\n\\nThe polycystic ovarian disease\xa0is an ovarian cause of abnormal uterine bleeding which comes under\xa0AUB-O,\xa0in the PALM-COEIN classification.\\n\\nAUB in the reproductive age group was previously known as dysfunctional uterine bleeding, however, that term is now obsolete. AUB can be acute or chronic:\\n\\n• Acute AUB - it can start sporadically or be superimposed on chronic AUB.\\n• Chronic AUB - it lasts for 6 months (3 months minimum).\\n\\nPALM-COEIN classification of AUB:\\n\\n• Polyp (AUB-P):\\n\\n• Endometrial polyps\\n• Endocervical polyps\\n\\n• Adenomyosis (AUB-A)\\n• Leiomyoma (AUB-L). Fibroids are further classified as:\\n\\n• Submucosal (SM)\\n• Other (O)\\n\\n• Malignancy and hyperplasia (AUB-M)\\n\\n• Endometrial hyperplasia\\n• Endometrial carcinoma\\n\\n• Coagulopathy (AUB-C)\\n\\n• von Willebrand\'s disease\\n• Hemophilias\\n\\n• Ovarian causes (AUB-O)\\n\\n• PCOD\\n• Hypothyroidism\\n• Hyperprolactinemia.\\n\\n• Endometrial causes (AUB-E)\\n\\n• Tubercular or chlamydial endometritis\\n\\n• Iatrogenic causes (AUB-I)\\n\\n• Intrauterine contraceptive devices\\n• Anticoagulants\\n• Tricyclic antidepressants\\n\\n• Not yet classified (AUB-N) includes rare causes such as\\n\\n• Arteriovenous malformations\\n• Myometrial hypertrophy","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":26,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A young female patient is undergoing evaluation for infertility and is posted for a hysterosalpingogram. Which of the following is not a contraindication for hysterosalpingography?","options":["Premenstrual phase","Uterine synechiae","Genital tract infection","Suspected pregnancy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Uterine synechiae\\n\\nAll of the following are contraindications for hysterosalpingography (HSG) except option C. Uterine synechiae is an indication for HSG.\\n\\nContraindications for HSG:\\n\\n• Presence of genital tract infection and bleeding\\n• Genital Tuberculosis, due to the risk of spreading infection.\\n• Suspected pregnancy\\n• Allergy to the dye\\n• Premenstrual phase\\n\\n• If by chance pregnancy has occurred, it may be dislodged.\\n• Thick endometrium might prevent the smooth flow of the dye, at the cornual end.\\n• There is a risk of embolism and endometriosis if HSG is done during this phase.\\n\\nHysterosalpingography (HSG) is an imaging technique used for visualization of the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes by the injection of a radiopaque dye.\\n\\nIt is best performed between day 5 and day 10 of the menstrual cycle.\\n\\n10-15ml of the dye is injected using a Leech-Wilkinson cannula, Rubin\'s cannula, or Foley\'s catheter. If the tubes are patent, the dye will spill out of the tubal ostia and smear the adjacent bowel.\\n\\nIt is used to detect:\\n\\n• Tubal patency\\n• Uterine anomalies\\n• Uterine polyps\\n• Uterine synechiae\\n• Feasibility of tuboplasty, by studying the location and extent of tubal pathology","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/32c12d1b901bcb46a9ae.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/5c523c4285b341b89a44.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"You have posted a 43-year-old woman with abnormal uterine bleeding for dilatation and curettage in the minor OT. Identify the following instrument used in this procedure.","options":["Sim\'s anterior vaginal wall retractor","Blake\'s uterine curette","Simpson\'s uterine sound","Sim\'s curette"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Sim\'s curette\\n\\nThe instrument shown in the image is Sim\'s curette.\xa0It is a single-ended uterine curette used for dilatation and curettage.\\n\\nIt is designed\xa0to remove tough tissue from the lining of the uterus for biopsy or excision. The curette features a sharp loop tip to aid in the removal of the uterine tissue.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/1dea9188c1db35dd6588.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/6d9e2fa37ccfd89f7844.jpg"}]},{"n":28,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 23-year-old woman suffers from emotional lability, fatigue, bloating, headache, sleeplessness, and breast tenderness about 10 days prior to her menses. These symptoms get relieved once she menstruates. All of the following are recommended in the management of this condition except:","options":["Calcium supplements","Fluoxetine","Caffeine","Vitamin B6"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Caffeine\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette is suggestive of\xa0Premenstrual syndrome (PMS). All the above-mentioned measures are recommended in the management of PMS except caffeine. Foods and beverages rich in sugar and caffeine can aggravate premenstrual symptoms in some women and are generally avoided.\\n\\nPremenstrual syndrome can be managed by dietary changes and lifestyle modifications like yoga, exercises and relaxation activities. Pharmacological therapy includes the usage of primrose oil, vitamin B1, vitamin B6, vitamin E, calcium and magnesium\xa0supplements.\\n\\nSymptoms can be alleviated by the use of prostaglandin inhibitors (ibuprofen and naproxen ),\xa0Selective Serotonin Reuptake inhibitors (SSRI) like fluoxetine,\xa0GABA\xa0antagonists and anxiolytics such as\xa0alprazolam. Danazol, bromocriptine and GnRH analogues can be used to reduce breast pain.\xa0OCPs with spironolactone and Mirena IUCD can also be used.\\n\\nPremenstrual Syndrome (PMS) is a symptom complex associated with ovulatory cycles. It occurs 7 to 14 days prior to menstruation and spontaneously resolves after menses. It occurs in the luteal phase of the cycle. When this symptom complex causes significant impairment, a diagnosis of premenstrual dysphoric disorder(PMDD)\xa0is made according to DSM-5.\\n\\nSymptoms include\\n\\n• Pain:- Headache, breast pain, abdominal cramps, muscle stiffness, backache and generalized body ache.\\n• Water retention:- Breast volume increase, bloating and weight gain.\\n• Behavioural changes:- Low performance, difficulty in concentration, irritability, depression, forgetfulness, low judgement, anxiety, loneliness, feeling like crying, hostility, increased appetite and cravings and\xa0sleeplessness.\\n• Autonomic changes:- Dizziness, faintness, nausea, vomiting and hot flushes.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"An incidental ovarian mass is found when evaluating a 19-year-old girl for kidney stones. The mass is removed and its contents are shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Immature teratoma","Mature teratoma","Struma ovarii","Granulosa cell tumor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Mature teratoma\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis in this patient with an asymptomatic ovarian mass consisting hair, bone, and sebaceous material is a\xa0mature teratoma.\\n\\nTeratomas are germ cell tumors. They contain embryonic tissues leading to the differentiation of cells along these pathways. There are three classes of teratomas:\\n\\n• Mature (benign) teratomas (eg: Dermoid cyst)\\n• Immature (essentially malignant) teratomas (eg: solid teratoma)\\n• Monodermal (highly specialized) tumors (eg:\xa0Struma ovarii)\\n\\nMature cystic teratoma,\xa0also known as dermoid cysts\xa0are unilocular in structure and have a smooth surface. They usually are lined by squamous epithelium with hair follicles leading to the formation of sebaceous material and hair. Other structures like teeth, bone, cartilage, thyroid tissue, and mucous membranes can also be found.\\n\\nMost patients are asymptomatic. Some patients may have symptoms because of the mass size of the teratoma. In other patients, ovarian teratomas can result in torsion of the ovary. Other rare complications include rupture of the cyst and spillage of contents into the abdominal cavity.\\n\\nTreatment of mature teratomas involves excision in order to prevent complications.\xa0Otherwise, mature teratomas are generally innocent.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A - Immature teratomas such as solid teratomas of the ovary are solid and trabeculated. They also contain cartilage, bone, hair, and sebaceous material. There is no structural order. They are generally malignant. Grossly, they have a smooth external surface and are solid on sectioning. There may be areas of necrosis or hemorrhage.\\n\\nThe following image shows both mature (left) and immature teratomas (right).\\n\\nOption C - Struma ovarii is a type of highly specialized teratoma that contains only thyroid tissue. If it is functioning, it may lead to hyperthyroidism.\\n\\nOption D - Granulosa cell tumors arise from the sex cords of the gonads. They are solid and generally produce estrogen. They can cause precocious puberty in children, hyperestrogenism in adults, and post-menopausal bleeding.\xa0They vary in appearance and range from microscopic to large, solid and cystic, encapsulated masses. If hormonally active, they may be yellow due to intracellular lipids.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/3a6473167857e8d36edf.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/239cdbdeb37b290d435a.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"You are examining an 18-year-old woman and it appears she has mature adult breasts. What is the Tanner stage of breast development in her?","options":["B5","B3","B4","B6"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. B5\\n\\nThe Tanner staging of the breast, in case of\xa0mature adult breast, is\xa0denoted as B5.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/779ef295860d5b8c50c4.jpg"}]},{"n":31,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A young nulliparous female who desires pregnancy comes to you with complaints of heavy menstrual bleeding. On palpation, an abdominal mass corresponding to 14 weeks of gestation is felt. The mass is found to be a subserosal pedunculated fibroid on imaging. What is the treatment of choice?","options":["Observation","Hysterectomy","Medical therapy","Myomectomy"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Myomectomy\\n\\nThe treatment of choice in this nulliparous female with\xa0subserosal pedunculated fibroid is myomectomy. Myomectomy is indicated if the uterus size corresponds to more than 12 weeks of gestation and the patient desires pregnancy.\\n\\nMyomectomy\xa0refers to the removal of fibroids and preserving the uterus. It is indicated in fibroids which present with the following\\n\\n• Persistent uterine bleeding despite medical therapy\\n• Excessive pain or pressure symptoms\\n• Size > 12 weeks, patient desirous to have a baby\\n• Unexplained infertility with distortion of the uterine cavity due to fibroid\\n• Recurrent pregnancy wastage due to fibroid\\n• Rapid growth during follow up\\n• Subserous pedunculated fibroid\\n\\nMyomectomy can be performed by laparoscopy, hysteroscopy, or laparotomy. It is contraindicated in patients with an infected fibroid, pelvic or endometrial tuberculosis, suspected malignancy,\xa0and growth in post-menopausal and multiparous women where hysterectomy can be the safe definitive management.\\n\\nFibroids are benign estrogen dependant tumors of the uterine smooth muscles and connective tissue.\\n\\nManagement of uterine fibroids in\xa0asymptomatic patients includes observation with regular follow-up\xa0regardless of the size of the fibroid. Surgery is indicated in\xa0cornual fibroids if it causes infertility or pedunculated fibroids (due to the risk of torsion).\\n\\nIn symptomatic patients,\xa0medical therapy is done to shrink the fibroid. It acts as a preoperative adjunct.\xa0Surgical methods such as myomectomy, laparoscopic myolysis, hysterectomy, and endometrial ablation are tried for patients with persistent symptoms despite conservative management. Hysterectomy is done in postmenopausal women and in multiparous women who have completed their families or are not desirous of childbirth.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":32,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Suitable cases for expectant management in case of placenta previa include all except:","options":["Assured fetal well-being","Duration of pregnancy >37 weeks","No active vaginal bleeding","Mother in good health"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Duration of pregnancy >37 weeks\\n\\nThe duration of pregnancy must be less than 37 weeks for expectant management which was advocated\xa0by\xa0McAfee\xa0and Johnson.\\n\\nThe aim of conservative management is to continue the pregnancy for fetal maturity without compromising maternal health.\\n\\nPrerequisites:\\n\\n• Availability of blood for transfusion\\n• Facilities for cesarean section available\\n\\nSelection of cases for expectant management for placenta\xa0previa:\\n\\n1. Mother is hemodynamically stable (Hb\xa0>10 g% and/or hematocrit\xa0>\xa030%)\\n2. Duration of pregnancy <37 weeks\\n3. No active vaginal bleeding\\n4. Fetus normal on USG and reactive on CTG\\n\\nConduct of expectant treatment:\\n\\n• Corticosteroids\xa0for fetal lung maturity\\n• Bed rest or pelvic rest\\n• Periodic monitoring of mother for bleeding pv\\n• Fetal surveillance with USG\\n• Tocolytics if contractions are present\\n• Anti-D to all\xa0Rh-negative (unsensitized) women.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":33,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Bleeding during normal menstruation occurs due to rupture of ____","options":["Radial artery","Basilar artery","Arcuate artery","Spiral artery"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Spiral artery\\n\\nRupture of the spiral arteries causes bleeding during normal menstruation. This is a branch of the uterine artery.\\n\\nThe\xa0blood supply\xa0of the uterus is as follows:\\n\\nUterine artery→Arcuate artery→Radial artery→Basal artery→Spiral artery","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/43262de287bc420d7347.jpg"}]},{"n":34,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A pregnant woman comes to you in her second stage of labor. On per vaginal examination, frontal sutures, anterior fontanelle, and supraorbital ridges are palpated. What could the fetal presentation be?","options":["Face","Vertex","Brow","Shoulder"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Brow\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario, the fetus could be in the brow presentation.\\n\\nOn per vaginal examination, the palpation of frontal sutures, anterior fontanelle, and supraorbital ridges indicate that the fetus is in the brow\xa0presentation. However, neither the mouth nor the chin can be palpated on per vaginal examination.\\n\\nThis is a rare presentation that is diagnosed when the portion of the fetal head between the orbital\xa0ridge and the anterior fontanel presents at the pelvic inlet.\\n\\nThe etiology of brow presentation includes:\\n\\n• Maternal: Multiparity with pendulous abdomen, lateral obliquity of the uterus, contracted pelvis.\\n• Fetal: Congenital malformations with the most common being anencephaly, congenital goiter, dolichocephalic head\\n\\nThe brow presentation is commonly unstable and frequently converts to a face or occipital presentation. Management is easy in the case of a small fetus and a large maternal pelvis. However, in the case of a large fetus, delivery by cesarean section is usually indicated.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Face presentation occurs when the neck is hyper-extended and the chin is the presenting part. The facial features may be palpable on per-vaginal examination.\\n\\nOption B: Vertex presentation occurs when the neck is well flexed. A quadrangular area of the fetal skull bound by the bregma, parietal eminence, and lambdoid sutures is palpable on per-vaginal examination.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Shoulder presentation can occur when the fetus is in the transverse lie. On per vaginal examination during labor, the shoulder is identified by palpating the following parts—the acromion process, the scapula, the clavicle, and the axilla. The characteristic landmarks are\\nthe feeling of the ribs and intercostal spaces (gridiron feel). On occasion, the arm is found prolapsed.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/60a9669d6e979cccaaab.jpg"}]},{"n":35,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A middle-aged woman complains of urine dribbling from the vagina. On further examination and investigation, she is diagnosed with a vesicovaginal fistula. Which of the following is false about the management of her condition?","options":["Latzko procedure is suited for post-hysterectomy fistula, caused by cancer cervix","Following a failed attempt, a second attempt can be made after 4 weeks","Continous bladder drainage required for 14 days postoperatively","Sexual abstinence for 3 months is advised after the surgery"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Following a failed attempt, a second attempt can be made after 4 weeks\\n\\nIn case the fistula repair fails, a second attempt is made only after 3 months.\\n\\nVesicovaginal fistula(VVF) is a type of\xa0genital fistula,\xa0which is an\xa0abnormal communication\xa0between the genital tract and the urinary tract. It\xa0occurs mostly due to\xa0obstetric injuries\xa0such as prolonged labor, and instrumental or manipulative deliveries. Patients present with\xa0true incontinence\xa0characterized by\xa0constant dribbling of urine.\\n\\nSmall fistulas due to difficult childbirth can heal spontaneously. In the case of an established fistula, it is better to wait for about\xa03 months\xa0for all tissue inflammation to subside, before surgery is undertaken.\xa0Most vesicovaginal fistulas are\xa0repaired per vaginally. The various surgeries done for the repair of VVF are as follows:\\n\\n• Latzko procedure: It is done by denudation of vaginal epithelium around fistulous edges and approximation of raw surface with absorbable sutures.\xa0This technique is suited for\xa0post-hysterectomy\xa0fistula that is caused by cancer cervix\xa0(Option A). It can lead to narrowing of the vagina or vaginal atresia. Fistula located at the vaginal vault, post hysterectomy is the most difficult to treat, this technique is suited for such cases.\\n• Chassar-Moir technique: Here, the vagina and bladder are separated and sutured into two layers taking care to avoid tension and overlapping suture lines.\\n• Ureteric implantation into the sigmoid colon in case previous surgeries fail.\\n\\nPost-operatively,\xa0continuous bladder drainage\xa0for 14 days\xa0(Option C) along with\xa0antibiotics\xa0is recommended.\xa0Vaginal\xa0or\xa0speculum examination\xa0and\xa0intercourse\xa0are not advised for\xa03 months\xa0after surgery (Option D). Avoiding pregnancy for a\xa0year\xa0is also recommended. A\xa0cesarean section\xa0is usually done for future pregnancies.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":36,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"The physiological changes occurring in the respiratory system during pregnancy include:\\n\\n1. A decrease in total lung capacity\\n2. A decrease in tidal volume\\n3. Increase in respiratory minute volume\\n4. Decrease in pulmonary resistance","options":["1, 2, 3, and 4","1, 3, and 4","1, 2, and 3","2, 3, and 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 3, and 4\\n\\nThe physiological changes occurring in the respiratory system during pregnancy include 1, 3, and 4. In pregnancy, the tidal volume is increased (not decreased).\\n\\nPhysiological changes in the respiratory system during pregnancy:\\n\\n• Decrease in total lung capacity: This occurs due to the elevation of the diaphragm by the enlarged uterus.\\n• Reduced pulmonary resistance\xa0due to the effect of progesterone.\\n• Increase in tidal volume\xa0and\xa0respiratory minute volume,\xa0due to the action of progesterone on the respiratory center and increased sensitivity of the respiratory center to CO2. As a result, the woman may feel shortness of breath.\\n• Increase\xa0in the\xa0subcostal\xa0angle\xa0from 68\xb0 to 103\xb0.\\n• Increase\xa0in the\xa0transverse\xa0diameter\xa0of the chest by 2 cm and\xa0chest\xa0circumference\xa0by 5-7 cm.\\n• Edema and hyperemia of the nasopharyngeal mucosa may occur leading to nasal stuffiness and rarely epistaxis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":37,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"The non-contraceptive uses of oral contraceptives include all of the following except:","options":["Reduction in the risk of ovarian cancer","Ectopic pregnancy","Protection against sexually transmitted diseases including HIV","Reduction in the risk of endometrial cancer"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Protection against sexually transmitted diseases including HIV\\n\\nThe non-contraceptive uses of oral contraceptives include all except protection against sexually transmitted diseases (STDs), including HIV.\\n\\nOral hormonal contraceptives can be\xa0combined oral contraceptive pills (COCPs) or progesterone-only\xa0minipills.\\n\\nCOCPs are a combination of ethinylestradiol\xa0at a dose of 20–30 mcg and an orally active progestogen. It is taken starting on the first day of the cycle for 21 days. They are taken at a fixed time of the day, preferably after a meal.\xa0Mala-N\xa0and Mala-D\xa0both contain levonorgestrel (0.15 mg) + ethinylestradiol (30 mcg). Mala-N is supplied free of cost\xa0in Family Planning Clinics in India. Each strip contains 21 hormonal tablets and 7 non-hormonal (iron) tablets.\\n\\nThe mechanism of action is the prevention of ovulation by\xa0suppression of the pituitary hormones\xa0FSH\xa0and LH.\xa0Progesterone also acts on the cervical mucus, making it thick and impenetrable by sperm. The pregnancy rate with the COCP is 0.3 per 100 woman-years.\\n\\nBenefits of the COCP:\\n\\n• It is effective in controlling fertility.\\n• It causes regular cycles and reduces menstrual blood loss and are, therefore, useful in\xa0menorrhagia and polymenorrhea.\\n• It lowers the incidence of benign breast neoplastic diseases, such as fibrocystic disease.\\n• It reduces the incidence of functional ovarian cysts and ovarian (Option A) and endometrial malignancy (Option D). The incidence of ovarian cancer is reduced by 40% and that of uterine malignancy, by 50%, if taken for 1 year; this effect lasts as long as 10 years after stoppage.\\n• It reduces the incidence of ectopic pregnancy (Option B) by suppressing ovulation and reducing the risk in pelvic inflammatory disease.\\n• It is useful in endometriosis, acne, and polycystic ovarian disease (PCOD).\\n\\nSide effects:\\n\\n• Higher incidence of breast cancer reported among users\\n• Suppression of lactation\\n• Occurrence of pituitary adenoma and adenoma of the liver\\n• Reduced carbohydrate tolerance (therefore, contraindicated in diabetic women)\\n• Migraine, depression, weight gain, and lethargy (due to progesterone)\\n• Increased risk of pulmonary embolism and cerebral thrombosis\\n\\nNinety-nine percent of women will regain normal menstrual cycles within 6 months of stopping the pills, while the return of fertility is slightly delayed.\\n\\nBarrier methods like condoms prevent the transmission of STDs.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":38,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 26-year-old primi developed fever and pruritic rashes one day after a full-term normal vaginal delivery. A diagnosis of varicella infection was made. How should the neonate be managed?","options":["Varicella-Zoster immunoglobulin to be given","Wait and watch for 1 week","Acyclovir therapy should be started immediately","Immediate vaccination against Varicella"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Varicella-Zoster immunoglobulin to be given\\n\\nPassive immunization with Varicella-Zoster immunoglobulin (VZIG) is administered to neonates whose mothers develop a varicella-zoster rash from five days before delivery up to two days after delivery.\\n\\nIf the fetus or neonate is exposed to active varicella infection just before or during delivery, they may develop a disseminated visceral and central nervous system disease, which can be fatal. Neonates born before or after this time are not at risk of severe neonatal chickenpox, and do not require passive immunization.\\n\\nVZIG may not prevent infection, but reduces the severity of neonatal infection. Monitoring of the neonate should be prolonged to 28 days, as VZIG may prolong the incubation period.\\n\\nIf signs of neonatal infection develop despite VZIG, the neonate should be treated with acyclovir.\\n\\nPrimary varicella infection presents with flu-like symptoms, followed by a pruritic vesicular rash. The fetus may develop congenital varicella syndrome if infection occurs in the first half of pregnancy, the highest risk is between 13 and 20 weeks. Features of congenital varicella syndrome include:\\n\\n• Chorioretinitis\\n• Microphthalmia\\n• Cerebral cortical atrophy\\n• Growth restriction\\n• Hydronephrosis\\n• Limb hypoplasia\\n• Cicatricial skin lesions\\n\\nPregnant women who are exposed to varicella and are susceptible should be given VZIG. Infected women usually require only supportive care, but those who require intravenous (IV) fluids and or develop pneumonia should be hospitalized. Intravenous acyclovir therapy is given to women requiring hospitalization.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":39,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 38-year-old woman presents with dysmenorrhoea, menorrhagia, and infertility. After ruling out other possible causes, the gynecologist suspects endometriosis. Which of the following investigations will be most helpful in the diagnosis?","options":["Hysteroscopy and endometrial biopsy","Transvaginal ultrasound","Laparoscopy","Hysterosalpingography"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Laparoscopy\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario, the investigation that will be most helpful in the diagnosis is\xa0laparoscopy.\\n\\nEndometriosis is the presence of endometrial tissue outside the uterus. The patient usually presents with symptoms of abdominal pain,\xa0dyspareunia,\xa0dysmenorrhea, and\xa0infertility.\xa0The presence of puckered spots in the posterior fornix is pathognomonic of endometriosis. It is an\xa0estrogen-dependent\xa0disease of the\xa0childbearing age\xa0group. The most common site of occurrence is the ovary, followed by the\xa0pouch of Douglas.\\n\\nThe gold standard for the diagnosis of endometriosis is visual inspection by\xa0laparoscopy,\xa0preferably with histological confirmation.\xa0Typically, there are small black dots, the so-called\xa0powder burns/tattooing\xa0seen on the uterosacral ligaments and pouch of Douglas.\\n\\nRole of laparoscopy in endometriosis:\\n\\n• To detect and diagnose pelvic endometriosis.\\n• Locate the site of endometriosis and staging.\\n• To take biopsy\\n• To surgically treat endometriosis by ablation, removal.\\n\\nOther investigations:\\n\\n• CA-125 levels are raised in endometriosis.\\n• Monocyte chemotactic protein (MCP-1) levels are raised in the peritoneal fluid of women with endometriosis.\\n• Transvaginal ultrasound reveals an echo-free cyst, low level echoes or clumps of high-density level echoes representing clots.\\n\\nThe image below shows\xa0powder burns/tattooing\xa0appearance of endometriosis on diagnostic laparoscopy.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Hysteroscopy and Endometrial Biopsy: These evaluate conditions within the uterine cavity, such as polyps or hyperplasia, but endometriosis involves ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus, making this investigation unhelpful for its diagnosis.\\n\\nOption B: Transvaginal Ultrasound (TVUS): While TVUS can detect ovarian endometriomas, it cannot reliably identify peritoneal or deep infiltrating endometriosis, limiting its utility in providing a comprehensive diagnosis.\\n\\nOption C: Hysterosalpingography (HSG): This test is primarily used to assess tubal patency and uterine contour in infertility workups but does not provide direct visualization of endometrial implants, making it unsuitable for diagnosing endometriosis.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/924dbb9b6be8856a4531.png"}]},{"n":40,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Which of the following parameters are included in the modified Biophysical profile score?\\n\\n1. Oxytocin challenge test\\n2. Contraction stress test\\n3. Amniotic fluid index\\n4. Vibroacoustic nonstress test\\n5. Amniotic fluid volume","options":["2, 4 and 5","3 and 4","1, 2 and 5","1, 3 and 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 3 and 4\\n\\nVibroacoustic nonstress test/Nonstress test (NST) and\xa0Amniotic fluid index (AFI) (options 3 and 4) are included in the\xa0modified biophysical score.\\n\\nBiophysical profile (BPP)/Manning score provides a means of accessing fetal health. Involves the measurement of the following: (Mnemonic: BATMaN)\\n\\n• B-Breathing\\n• A-Amniotic fluid volume (AFV)\\n• T-Tone of fetus\\n• M-Movements of fetus\\n• N-Non Stress Test (NST)\\n\\nNormal score: >=8 (with normal AFV). The maximum score is 10.\\n\\nAbnormal scores:\\n\\n• 8/10 with decreased AFV: suspicion of chronic fetal asphyxia\\n• 6/10: Possible fetal asphyxia\\n• 4/10: Probable fetal asphyxia\\n• 0-2/10: Almost certain fetal asphyxia\\n\\nMaternal sedatives and narcotics can significantly lower the score.\\n\\nThe biophysical profile was modified to includes two parameters- the biweekly vibroacoustic nonstress test or Nonstress test and the AFI.\\n\\nThe nonstress test describes fetal heart rate acceleration in response to fetal movements, using a Doppler to detect fetal heart rate acceleration. Fetal movements are recorded as perceived by the mother.\xa0Acoustic stimulation nonstress test is one in which a loud external sound is used to startle the fetus and provoke heart rate accelerations.\\n\\n• Normal modified BPP: Reactive nonstress test with AFI>5cm\\n• Abnormal modified BPP: nonreactive nonstress test or AFI <5 cm\\n\\nOxytocin challenge test (Contraction stress test): The fetal heart rate and uterine contractions are recorded simultaneously with an external monitor. If at least three spontaneous contractions of 40 seconds or longer are present in 10 minutes, uterine stimulation is not required. If required, it can be done with iv oxytocin or nipple stimulation.\\n\\nA positive test(abnormal) is uniform repetitive late fetal heart rate decelerations, which could indicate uteroplacental insufficiency. A negative test would show no late or significant variable decelerations.\\n\\nThe nonstress test is a test of fetal condition, while the contraction stress test is a test of uteroplacental function.\\n\\nContraindications of contraction stress testing:\\n\\n• Compromised fetus\\n• Previous history of cesarean section\\n• Complications likely to produce preterm labor\\n• Antepartum hemorrhage\\n• Multiple pregnancies","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":41,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Match the engaging diameters marked on the fetal head with the presentations they correspond to.","options":["1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d","1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c","1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a","1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c\\n\\nThe correct matching is 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c.\\n\\nIn the above figure,\\n\\n• 1 represents the submento-bregmatic diameter\\n• 2 represents the mento-vertical diameter\\n• 3 represents the occipitofrontal diameter\\n• 4 represents the suboccipito-bregmatic diameter\\n\\nThe engaging diameters of various presentations are:\\n\\nDiameters\\nMeasurement in cm (inches)\\nThe attitude of the head\\nPresentation\\n\\nSuboccipitobregmatic\\n9.5 cm\\nComplete flexion\\nVertex\\n\\nSuboccipito-frontal\\n10 cm\\nIncomplete flexion\\nVertex\\n\\nOccipitofrontal\\n11.5 cm\\nMarked deflexion\\nVertex\\n\\nMento-vertical\\n14 cm\\nPartial extension\\nBrow\\n\\nSubmentovertical\\n11.5 cm\\nIncomplete extension\\nFace\\n\\nSubmentobregmatic\\n9.5 cm\\nComplete extension\\nFace","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/65333cdeea1509a9fe75.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/881cfede6bd5786e800d.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b72ed2d240381afe00b8.jpg"}]},{"n":42,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"An anxious pregnant woman came to you for a second opinion with the report of her biochemical screening tests. An increased level of human chorionic gonadotropin(hCG), as compared to normal pregnancy hCG levels, can be seen in all of the following conditions except:","options":["Hydatidiform mole","Ectopic pregnancy","Trisomy 21 fetus","Multiple pregnancy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ectopic pregnancy\\n\\nThe levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) are decreased in ectopic pregnancy when compared to the level of hCG in normal intrauterine pregnancy.\\n\\nIn comparison to hCG levels in normal pregnancy, increased levels of hCG are found in:\\n\\n• Hydatidiform mole or choriocarcinoma\\n• Trisomy 21 fetus\\n• Multiple pregnancies\\n\\nDecreased levels of hCG as compared to\xa0normal pregnancy hCG levels are found in:\\n\\n• Ectopic pregnancy\\n• Spontaneous abortion\\n\\nHuman chorionic gonadotropin\xa0is a glycoprotein. It can be\xa0detected in the maternal serum or urine\xa0as early as 8–9 days postfertilization. Maximum levels in blood and urine are found between\xa060 - 70 days of\xa0pregnancy. It\xa0disappears from the circulation within 2 weeks following delivery.\\n\\nhCG is chemically and functionally similar to the pituitary luteinizing hormone (LH). The\xa0α subunit is biochemically similar to LH, FSH (follicular stimulating hormone), and TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone) whereas the\xa0β subunit is relatively unique to hCG.\\n\\nFunctions of hCG are:\\n\\n• It stimulates the secretion of progesterone by the corpus luteum during pregnancy\\n• Rescue\xa0and maintenance of corpus luteum till 6 weeks of pregnancy\\n• It stimulates Leydig cells of the male fetus to produce testosterone in conjunction with fetal\xa0pituitary gonadotropins\\n• It inhibits the maternal processes of\xa0immune rejection of the fetus as a homograft.\\n• Stimulates both adrenal and placental steroidogenesis.\\n• Stimulates maternal thyroid because of its thyrotropic activity.\\n• Promotes secretion of relaxin from the corpus luteum.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":43,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"Menopause is defined as cessation of menses for a period of _____.","options":["12 months","3 months","9 months","6 months"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 12 months\\n\\nMenopause is defined as cessation of ovarian function\xa0for 12 months resulting in permanent amenorrhoea. It is a retrospective diagnosis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":44,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A mother of a newborn noticed vaginal discharge on her sixth-day postpartum. It was explained to the patient that the discharge was called lochia, and it is a normal finding postpartum. Arrange the correct order of the appearance of lochia.","options":["Rubra- Alba- serosa","Serosa- Rubra -Alba","Rubra- Serosa- Alba","Serosa-Alba-Rubra"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Rubra- Serosa- Alba\\n\\nThe correct order of appearance of lochia is rubra-serosa-alba.\\n\\nLochia\xa0refers to the vaginal discharge resulting from the sloughing of decidual tissue in the early puerperal period.\\n\\nName\\nColour\\nTimings\xa0after delivery\\nContents\\n\\nLochia\xa0Rubra\\nRed\\nFirst few days\\nBlood, decidua, fetal membranes, Vernix, lanugo, meconium\\n\\nLochia\xa0Serosa\\nYellowish/pink\\nAfter 3 to 4 days\\nWBCs, cervical mucus, micro-organisms\\n\\nLochia\xa0Alba\\nPale White\\nAfter 10th day\\nDecidua, cells, WBC, mucin, cholesterin crystals and fat cells\\n\\nThe average amount of discharge for the first week is\xa0250 mL\xa0and may persist for about 3 weeks.\\n\\nMalodorous lochia may signify underlying infection. Copious or absent lochia may also indicate infection or lochiometra. The persistence of lochia rubra beyond the normal limit may signify subinvolution or retained bits of the conceptus. Prolonged discharge of lochia alba for more than 3 weeks may signify local genital lesion.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":45,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"According to DeLancey\'s levels of support of the genital organs, uterosacral ligaments belong to which level of pelvic support?","options":["Level 4","Level 2","Level 1","Level 3"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Level 1\\n\\nAccording to DeLancey, the uterosacral ligaments and cardinal ligaments belong to level 1.\\n\\nThis dense visceral connective tissue complex maintains\xa0the vaginal length and horizontal axis. It allows the vagina to\xa0be supported by the levator plate and positions the cervix just\xa0superior to the level of the ischial spines. Defects in this support\xa0complex may lead to apical prolapse.\\n\\nLevel I support suspends the proximal vagina. Level II support attaches the midvagina along its length to the arcus tendineus fascia. Level III support results from the fusion of the distal vagina with adjacent structures.\\n\\nPubococcygeus\xa0is the deepest part of the levator ani muscle. Injury to it\xa0can cause enterocele, cystocele, and urethral descent.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/805e54c4ccb8730a0cb0.jpg"}]},{"n":46,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A resident is doing an abdominal examination in a primigravida. Sequentially arrange the respective uses of Leopold\'s First maneuver, Leopold\'s second maneuver, Leopold\'s third maneuver, and Leopold\'s fourth maneuver in the corresponding order\\n\\n1. Determine the degree of descent\\n2. Confirmation of fetal presentation\\n3. Determine the fetal orientation\\n4. Determine which fetal pole occupies the fundus.","options":["4-1-2-3","1-2-3-4","4-3-2-1","4-2-3-1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 4-3-2-1\\n\\nThe correct sequence is 4, 3, 2, 1:\\n\\nLeopold First maneuvre -\xa0Determine which fetal pole occupies the fundus\\n\\nLeopold second maneuver -\xa0Determine the fetal orientation\\n\\nLeopold third maneuver -\xa0Confirmation of fetal presentation\\n\\nLeopold fourth maneuver -\xa0Determine the degree of descent\\n\\nLeopold’s first maneuver (fundal grip) is used to determine which fetal pole\xa0occupies the fundus.\\n\\nLeopold’s second maneuver (lateral grip) can be used to determine the fetal orientation based on whether the back is facing anteriorly, transversely, or posteriorly.\xa0The palms are placed on either side of the maternal abdomen and gentle deep pressure is exerted. The fetal back is felt as a hard-resistant structure and the fetal limbs are felt as multiple small irregular parts.\\n\\nLeopold’s third maneuver (Pawlik’s grip)\xa0helps to confirm fetal presentation. It is done by grasping the lower part of the maternal abdomen with the thumb and fingers of one hand. The palpation of a hard and round mass indicates that the presenting part is the fetal head. A movable mass suggests that the presenting part is not engaged. If the presenting part is breech, it is felt as a large nodular mass.\\n\\nLeopold’s fourth maneuver (pelvic grip) helps to\xa0determine the degree of descent. The examiner faces the mother\'s feet, and the fingertips of both hands are positioned on either side of the presenting part. They exert inward pressure and then slide along the axis of the pelvic inlet.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/94923c4ecb8022f798de.jpg"}]},{"n":47,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"All of the following findings indicate uterine scar rupture, except ______.","options":["Maternal bradycardia","Fetal bradycardia","Loss of station of presenting part","Hematuria"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Maternal bradycardia\\n\\nMaternal bradycardia is not a feature of uterine rupture.\\n\\nUterine rupture is one in which all the layers of the uterus (endometrium, myometrium, and serosa ) are disrupted any time after 28 weeks of pregnancy.\\n\\nA previous history of classical cesarean section, obstructed labor, manual removal of placenta, dilatation, and curettage are some risk factors for uterine scar rupture.\\n\\nClinical features of\xa0scar dehiscence:\\n\\n• Maternal tachycardia\\n• Fetal bradycardia\\n• Maternal hypotension\\n• Uterine scar tenderness\\n• Bleeding per vagina\\n• Hematuria\\n• Loss of station of presenting part\\n\\nManagement\xa0of uterine scar rupture includes resuscitation and laparotomy.\xa0Following resuscitative measures and laparotomy,\xa0subtotal\xa0hysterectomy is usually done unless there is sufficient reason to preserve the uterus.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":48,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A 30-year-old primigravida who conceived dichorionic diamniotic twins presented at 38 weeks of gestation. On examination, the first twin was found to be in cephalic presentation and the second twin in breech presentation. All of the following can be undertaken for delivery except?","options":["C-section to deliver both twins","Vaginal delivery of 1st twin, internal podalic version of 2nd twin","Vaginal delivery of 1st twin, breech extraction of 2nd twin","Vaginal delivery of 1st twin, external cephalic version and delivery of 2nd twin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Vaginal delivery of 1st twin, internal podalic version of 2nd twin\\n\\nAll of the above-mentioned measures can be undertaken in this case except vaginal delivery of 1st twin and internal podalic version of 2nd twin.\\n\\nThe internal podalic version is an obstetric procedure in which the baby is turned inside the uterus such that the feet present through the cervix. Upward pressure is applied by the abdominal hand on the head while downward traction is exerted on the feet and the baby is then delivered by breech extraction.\xa0It is indicated when the second twin is in a transverse lie, not breech. This procedure is always done under general or epidural anesthesia.\\n\\nWhen the first twin is cephalic and the second is non-cephalic, the following methods can be used:\\n\\n• Breech extraction of the second twin\\n• C-section of both twins\\n• An external cephalic version of the second twin\\n• Rarely, cesarean section of the second twin following vaginal delivery of the first\\n\\nThe ACOG recommends termination of pregnancy at 38 weeks of pregnancy in dichorionic gestations. Cesarean section is preferred when the first twin is in a breech position. An attempted vaginal delivery can cause locking of the fetuses, trapping of the fetal head above the cervix, and umbilical cord prolapse.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":49,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"The instrument shown in the image is used for:","options":["Correcting asynctilism","Delivery of occipitotransverse position in platypelloid pelvis","Delivery of after coming head in breech delivery","Outlet forceps used to provide traction force"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Outlet forceps used to provide traction force\\n\\nThe image shows\xa0Wrigley\'s forceps, which is an outlet forceps used to provide traction force.\\n\\nWrigley\'s forceps\xa0are used exclusively in outlet forceps operations for the extraction of the head. It is used to provide traction force. The instrument is light and short due to a reduction in the length of the shanks and handles. It has a marked cephalic curve with a slight pelvic curve.\xa0Outlet forceps are used when:\\n\\n• the fetal scalp is visible without separating the labia\\n• the fetal skull has reached the pelvic floor, at or on the perineum\\n• the sagittal suture is in the anteroposterior diameter or right or left occiput anterior or posterior position (rotation does not exceed 45\xb0)\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Kielland forceps\xa0are used in correcting asynctilism.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Barton forceps\xa0are\xa0used for delivery of occipitotransverse positions in a platypelloid pelvis.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Piper forceps\xa0have a distinct perineal curve and allows for application to the\xa0after-coming head in breech delivery.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/b494750ab1528bf25162.jpg"]},{"n":50,"subject":"Obstetrics & Gynaecology","text":"A postmenopausal woman presents with irregular bleeding. Endometrial biopsy revealed endometrial hyperplasia without atypia. How would you consider treating this patient?","options":["Norethisterone","MIRENA IUD","Total hysterectomy","Danazol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Norethisterone\\n\\nProgesterone therapy such as norethisterone followed by endometrial biopsy every 3 to 6 months is the best treatment in the case of endometrial hyperplasia without atypia.\\n\\nEndometrial hyperplasia\xa0is defined as endometrial thickening with the proliferation of irregularly sized and shaped glands and an increased gland-to-stroma ratio. It is a\xa0pre-malignant\xa0lesion for endometrial carcinoma.\\n\\nWHO Classification of endometrial hyperplasia based on histology:\\n\\n• Simple without atypia:\xa0there is an increase in number of glands but the architecture is not compromised.\\n• Simple with atypia: simple hyperplasia with features of nuclear atypia (variation in nuclear shape, size, and prominent nucleoli)\\n• Complex without atypia:\xa0glands are markedly crowded and irregular\\n• Complex with atypia: complex hyperplasia with atypical nuclear features.\\n\\nHowever, there is generally no clear histological distinction between the above mentioned classification, so endometrial hyperplasia is classified into hyperplasia without atypia and atypical hyperplasia/endometrioid intraepithelial neoplasia.\\n\\nType of hyperplasia\\nRisk of progression to cancer\\n\\nSimple without atypia\\n1%\\n\\nComplex without atypia\\n3%\\n\\nSimple with atypia\\n8%\\n\\nComplex with atypia\\n29%\\n\\nManagement of Endometrial hyperplasia:\\n\\n1) Hyperplasia without atypia:\\n\\nIntrauterine or continuous oral progesterone. Levonorgestrel releasing intrauterine system is preferred due to its lower side effect profile, higher rate of disease resolution. Oral progesterone can be given in the form of medroxyprogesterone acetate 10-20 mg/day.\\n\\nEndometrial surveillance in the form of biopsy should be performed at 6-month intervals. At least 2 consecutive negative biopsies should be obtained. Those at high risk (body mass index of more than 35, treated with oral progesterone) should be advised annual follow-up.\\n\\n2) Atypical hyperplasia:\\n\\nTotal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is suggested in those with atypical hyperplasia due to the risk of progression to malignancy.\\n\\nPremenopausal women who want to preserve their fertility should be counseled regarding the risk of progression to cancer. Once fertility is no longer required, then hysterectomy should be offered.\\n\\nThose who refuse hysterectomy should have routine endometrial biopsies every 3 months until 2 consecutive negative biopsies are obtained.","img":false,"explImg":false}]}')}}]);